The three NSOpenGL global option calls, which an application needs whether or not it draws.

libswiftAppKit.dylib binds NSOpenGLGetOption, NSOpenGLSetOption and NSOpenGLGetVersion, EXPECTED IN
AppKit, so any application that loads the Swift AppKit overlay dies on the first of them. iTerm2
did, and nothing about the symptom said so: a lazy bind abort is signal 6 at a dyld frame naming
nothing. The header did not even declare them, so no caller in this tree could have been compiled
against them either.

WHAT YOU SET IS WHAT YOU GET is all these can honestly promise here. There is no renderer library
behind them to cache pixel formats for or to reset, so the two cache actions are no-ops, the three
settings round trip, and the defaults are the documented macOS ones so a caller that reads before
writing sees a sane cache size rather than zero. NSOpenGLGetVersion answers the NSOpenGL API
version, 1.0, which is not the OpenGL version and never was.

Task #228.

--- a/AppKit/include/AppKit/NSOpenGL.h
+++ b/AppKit/include/AppKit/NSOpenGL.h
@@ -4,3 +4,18 @@
 #import <AppKit/NSOpenGLPixelBuffer.h>
 #import <AppKit/NSOpenGLPixelFormat.h>
 #import <AppKit/NSOpenGLView.h>
+
+/* The three global option calls. libswiftAppKit.dylib binds all three EXPECTED IN AppKit, so an
+ * application that loads the Swift overlay dies on the first of them whether or not it ever draws
+ * with OpenGL: iTerm2 died opening a menu. Task #228. */
+typedef enum {
+    NSOpenGLGOFormatCacheSize = 501,
+    NSOpenGLGOClearFormatCache = 502,
+    NSOpenGLGORetainRenderers = 503,
+    NSOpenGLGOResetLibrary = 504,
+    NSOpenGLGOUseBuildCache = 506,
+} NSOpenGLGlobalOption;
+
+APPKIT_EXPORT void NSOpenGLSetOption(NSOpenGLGlobalOption pname, GLint param);
+APPKIT_EXPORT void NSOpenGLGetOption(NSOpenGLGlobalOption pname, GLint *param);
+APPKIT_EXPORT void NSOpenGLGetVersion(GLint *major, GLint *minor);
